erview\nDoctoral training programmes\nExternal funding and loans\nColleges\nColleges overview\nCollege listing\nAccommodation\nCollege Visit Days\nApplying\nApplying overview\nApplication deadlines\nBefore you apply\nEntry requirements\nInternational qualifications\nEnglish language requirements\nFind a supervisor\nWidening access and participation\nHow to apply\nSupporting documents\nReferences\nApplication fee\nInternational applications\nDisabled applicants\nVisiting students\nTransferring from another university\nAI and postgraduate applications\nAdmissions fraud\nAfter you apply\nHow we assess your application\nInterviews\nOutcome of your application\nYour offer\nChanging your start date to a later term\nDeferring your offer to the next academic year\nPrepare to arrive\nTrack your application\nInternational\nInternational overview\nHow to apply\nInternational qualifications\nEnglish language requirements\nVisas and immigration\nInternational visits and events\nStudent views\nAkhila\u2019s story\nAlex\u2019s story\nHuijie\u2019s story\nKelsey\u2019s story\nLu\u2019s story\nNilesh\u2019s story\nGet in touch!\nEvents\nEvents overview\nPostgraduate Open Days\nDiscover Cambridge webinars\nInternational visits and events\nGetting here\nContact us\nPostgraduate qualifications\nMain menu\nCourses\nCourse Directory\nDepartment directory\nQualification types\nPart-time study\nWhy Cambridge\nFinance\nFunding\nColleges\nApplying\nInternational\nEvents\nContact us\nCambridge offers a range of qualifications from Doctoral programmes and Master\u2019s degrees to Postgraduate Certificates and Diplomas.\nYou can explore our full list of courses in the Course Directory. Use keywords and filter options to narrow your search.\nTypes of qualifications we offer\nFind out about the types of postgraduate courses we offer and which is the best option for you.\nDoctor of Philosophy (PhD)\nA PhD is the University's main and highest level research degree.\nAs a PhD student, you'll focus on producing independent and original research that makes a significant contribution to knowledge in your field of study. You can either propose your own research topic or apply to work on a pre-defined research project, like a studentship.\nAssessment is by submission of a thesis and an oral examination (viva).\nA PhD normally takes 3-4 years full-time (or 4-7 years part-time) to complete, including a probationary first year. Progression at the end of the probation is not automatic. You\u2019ll need to pass a formal progress assessment to continue.\nMost applicants will have completed at least 1 year of postgraduate study, usually a research-focused Master\u2019s degree, before starting a PhD.\nMaster of Philosophy (MPhil)\nCambridge offers two types of MPhil:\nMPhil by Advanced Study, which includes taught elements\nMPhil by Research/Thesis, which is entirely research-based\nMPhils are typically studied over 9\u201312 months full-time or 2 years part-time. Most taught MPhils are only available full-time.\nBoth routes provide an excellent transition to PhD study by developing research skills and specialist knowledge. The MPhil dissertation is also a valuable introductory experience for doctoral research.\nFor some subjects, you need an MPhil to progress to a PhD.\nMaster of Research (MRes)\nThe MRes is a research programme specifically designed to prepare students for doctoral study (PhD) or a career in research.\nIt focuses on developing research skills and methodologies, and combines taught modules, research skills training, and a substantial independent research component.\nThe MRes is a 1-year full-time course, but at Cambridge it is usually offered only as part of a 4-year combined doctoral programme. Continuation to the PhD is subject to satisfactory performance in the MRes and the identification of a suitable topic and supervisor.\nMaster of Advanced Study (MASt)\nThe MASt is a 9-month, full-time Master's course, designed as a top-up for students who already hold a 3-year undergraduate degree.\nIt is the equivalent of a fourth undergraduate year, and students work alongside fourth-year (Part III) students taking the final year of an integrated Master's.\nMaster of Studies (MSt)\nThe MSt is a flexible, part-time Master\u2019s degree designed to be completed alongside existing commitments, including full-time work. This enables professionals to develop their interests for career progression, diversification or personal development.\nThe programme is usually 2 years and contains a significant taught element through modular teaching blocks, and a research project that may be work-related, if appropriate.\nThe flexible format also makes them accessible to students living outside Cambridge, including international students - although students are usually required to attend residentials in Cambridge during the programme.\nOther qualifications\nOther courses available for postgraduate study at the University of Cambridge include:\nAdvanced Diploma (AdvDip)\nExecutive Master of Business Administration (EMBA)\nMaster of Business Administration (MBA)\nMaster of Corporate Law (MCL)\nDoctor of Medicine (MD)\nDoctor of Education (EdD)\nMaster of Finance (MFin)\nMaster of Accounting (MAcc)\nMaster of Architecture (MArch)\nMaster of Law (LLM)\nMaster of Education (MEd)\nMaster of Music (MMus)\nMaster of Surgery (MChir)\nPostgraduate Advanced Certificate (PGACert)\nPostgraduate Award (PGA)\nPostgraduate Certificate (PGCert)\nPostgraduate Diploma (PGDip)\nBrowse the Course Directory for full details about our courses.\nRelated Links\nPhD by Special Regulations\nCambridge and credits\nTransferring from another university\nPostgraduate Admissions Office\nAbout us\nAdmissions statistics\nStart an application\nApplicant Self-Service\nContact us\nAbout this site\nAbout our website\nCookie notice\nPrivacy policy\nEvent booking policy\nUniversity Policy and Guidelines\nUniversity privacy policy\nInformation compliance\nEquality and Diversity\nT